WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.586
Welcome to Day 2 of Case Management Training.

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:08.249
Yesterday we opened John Doe versus Robert Smith and carried it through intake and

00:00:08.249 --> 00:00:11.878
treatment, including the fourteen-day gap and the near-drop in April.

00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:14.759
Today we follow that same file to the money.

00:00:15.109 --> 00:00:19.967
We audit it before anything leaves the office, we build and serve the Bodily Injury

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:24.357
demand to Keystone, we work the negotiation, and we close the BI settlement

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:24.884
properly.

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:29.356
Keep John's file open in your mind, because every lesson today lands on it.

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:33.241
Today has three parts.

00:00:33.591 --> 00:00:38.204
First, the pre-demand audit, where we test the whole file the way an adjuster would

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:39.872
before a single page goes out.

00:00:40.222 --> 00:00:44.750
Second, the Bodily Injury demand itself, which on our file is the Time-Limited

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.884
Demand to Keystone for the ten thousand dollar limits.

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:52.955
Third, the BI settlement, where we negotiate, handle the consents and the release,

00:00:52.955 --> 00:00:55.200
and move the file from settled to paid.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.026
Each part builds on the one before it, so the quality of your audit decides how the

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.158
rest of the day goes.

00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:06.250
Let's talk about why the audit comes first.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.260
Some firms call it a forensic case audit or a pre-litigation audit,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.520
but the idea is the same: you vet the evidence and the value before the demand

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.285
letter exists.

00:01:15.635 --> 00:01:17.817
The slide gives four objectives.

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:22.068
Verification asks whether what we say matches what the records show.

00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:25.530
Valuation asks whether every dollar is justified.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.663
Risk assessment asks where the defense will push back, such as comparative fault.

00:01:31.013 --> 00:01:33.822
Compliance asks whether we are inside every deadline.

00:01:34.172 --> 00:01:36.471
On our file, the audit earns its keep.

00:01:36.821 --> 00:01:41.937
Summit Imaging's MRI history gave the injury date as February 16th instead of

00:01:41.937 --> 00:01:45.923
February 6th, Riverton Spine and Rehab billed May 4th twice,

00:01:45.923 --> 00:01:50.707
and Crescent's statement wrongly billed John the contractual adjustment.

00:01:51.057 --> 00:01:57.256
Those were caught here, and three Amendment Requests went out on July 6th, 2026.

00:01:57.606 --> 00:02:03.841
And the statute of limitations, docketed for February 6th, 2028, was confirmed.

00:02:04.191 --> 00:02:06.443
That's the sanity check doing its job.

00:02:08.993 --> 00:02:10.723
This slide is about alignment.

00:02:11.073 --> 00:02:15.601
The goal is that when the demand lands on the adjuster's desk, there is no easy out.

00:02:15.951 --> 00:02:18.052
Three pillars have to tell the same story.

00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:20.899
The narrative is the client's account of what happened.

00:02:21.248 --> 00:02:24.848
The physicality is the property damage and the force of the impact.

00:02:25.198 --> 00:02:27.044
The medicals are the clinical proof.

00:02:27.394 --> 00:02:30.509
If one pillar contradicts the others, value collapses,

00:02:30.509 --> 00:02:33.164
because the adjuster will pull on that thread.

00:02:33.514 --> 00:02:34.988
So let's test our file.

00:02:35.338 --> 00:02:37.820
The narrative: John was the front-seat passenger,

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:41.770
and Robert Smith rolled through the all-way stop and struck the Escape's front

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.353
passenger door at about twenty-eight miles an hour,

00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:46.379
pushing it into Margaret's parked Civic.

00:02:46.729 --> 00:02:50.735
The physicality matches: a door-side impact on John's side.

00:02:51.085 --> 00:02:55.919
And the medicals match: EMS noted neck stiffness on the day of the crash,

00:02:55.919 --> 00:02:58.701
and the MRI found a C5-C6 disc herniation.

00:02:59.051 --> 00:03:00.955
Three pillars, one story.

00:03:01.305 --> 00:03:03.174
That's what the audit is checking.

00:03:05.724 --> 00:03:06.571
Now the math.

00:03:06.921 --> 00:03:10.381
The checklist starts with financial and lien verification.

00:03:10.731 --> 00:03:14.713
Special damages are the hard numbers: medical bills and lost wages.

00:03:15.063 --> 00:03:18.552
The slide's general definition also folds in property damage,

00:03:18.552 --> 00:03:22.326
but on our file the Civic is Margaret's, handled on her own claim,

00:03:22.326 --> 00:03:24.328
so it stays out of John's specials.

00:03:24.678 --> 00:03:28.643
Our numbers are medical specials of fourteen thousand eight hundred seventeen

00:03:28.643 --> 00:03:32.608
dollars and forty cents after the audit, plus lost wages of one thousand five

00:03:32.608 --> 00:03:36.933
hundred eighty-four dollars, for total specials of sixteen thousand four hundred one

00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:38.169
dollars and forty cents.

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:42.455
The slide warns that wages need an employer letter and a doctor's note.

00:03:42.805 --> 00:03:45.312
We have Northgate's wage-and-attendance letter,

00:03:45.312 --> 00:03:49.472
and each missed shift is tied to a record, except five shifts in February that

00:03:49.472 --> 00:03:50.432
predate treatment.

00:03:50.782 --> 00:03:54.660
Those were flagged to Atty. Thorne and kept in the claim, never hidden.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:55.927
Then liens.

00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.782
Peach State first asserted three thousand six hundred eighteen dollars,

00:03:59.782 --> 00:04:03.533
and the line-by-line dispute brought it to one thousand eight hundred sixty.

00:04:03.883 --> 00:04:07.123
Riverton Spine and Rehab's LOP is the other.

00:04:07.473 --> 00:04:10.700
Miss a lien, and the client can walk away with nothing.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.096
Next is liability proof.

00:04:15.446 --> 00:04:19.951
The point here is that liability is never assumed just because someone got a ticket.

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:24.280
You verify the final police report, not just the exchange of information,

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.824
and you read it for citations and any note of contributory fault.

00:04:28.174 --> 00:04:34.580
On our file, Riverton PD report R P D 26 018842 cites Robert Smith for failure to

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.162
stop at a stop sign.

00:04:36.512 --> 00:04:41.182
For witnesses, the slide says confirm the contact details still work and watch for

00:04:41.182 --> 00:04:44.372
statement drift, meaning a story that changes over time.

00:04:44.722 --> 00:04:49.756
Our independent witness is Ruth Calloway, who lives at 2209 Maple Hollow Road and

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:52.988
gave a signed statement to the officer at the scene.

00:04:53.338 --> 00:04:56.333
Then the scene audit, checking that the physics make sense.

00:04:56.683 --> 00:05:00.863
The slide mentions Google Earth or dashcam footage as general tools.

00:05:01.213 --> 00:05:04.004
We have something better: Margaret's doorbell camera,

00:05:04.004 --> 00:05:07.006
which shows the Escape stopped and Smith rolling through.

00:05:07.356 --> 00:05:11.536
That copy was secured on February 19th, 2026.

00:05:14.086 --> 00:05:16.442
Third, the property damage deep dive.

00:05:16.792 --> 00:05:19.407
You audit the photos for a clear point of impact,

00:05:19.407 --> 00:05:23.729
look for frame or undercarriage damage, compare the first estimate with the final

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:27.624
shop bill, and look for supplements that reveal hidden structural damage.

00:05:27.974 --> 00:05:32.502
If a car is totaled, you check the valuation report so the owner isn't lowballed.

00:05:32.852 --> 00:05:35.360
On our file there's a twist worth understanding.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.481
The damaged car in the property claim is Margaret's 2019 Civic,

00:05:39.481 --> 00:05:43.791
not John's, and the firm handles it under a separate PD-only engagement.

00:05:44.141 --> 00:05:46.184
Still, it tells the force story.

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:49.802
The first estimate was six thousand nine hundred forty dollars,

00:05:49.802 --> 00:05:53.743
and the teardown supplement added two thousand three hundred fifteen for the

00:05:53.743 --> 00:05:55.299
A-pillar, which is structural.

00:05:55.649 --> 00:05:59.086
The final repair was nine thousand two hundred fifty-five dollars.

00:05:59.436 --> 00:06:03.279
Against an actual cash value of seventeen thousand six hundred,

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:07.060
that's about fifty-three percent, so the Civic was repairable,

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.097
not a total loss.

00:06:10.647 --> 00:06:15.616
Fourth, the medical and billing audit, and this is where our file is tested hardest.

00:06:15.966 --> 00:06:18.160
Build the chronology and read it for gaps.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.260
The slide's rule is that a fourteen-day gap needs a documented reason.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:26.012
Ours is exactly that: the crash was February 6th,

00:06:26.012 --> 00:06:29.135
and John's first treatment was February 20th.

00:06:29.485 --> 00:06:33.093
The reasons were no transportation, thinking it was soreness,

00:06:33.093 --> 00:06:36.050
worry about cost, and then numbness into his hand,

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.776
and they're corroborated by the EMS report, his work call-outs,

00:06:39.776 --> 00:06:42.674
his texts to his mother and the pharmacy receipt.

00:06:43.024 --> 00:06:46.043
There's also the nineteen-day disruption in April,

00:06:46.043 --> 00:06:48.156
explained by crash-related anxiety.

00:06:48.506 --> 00:06:51.745
Next, the coding audit for unbundling or duplicates.

00:06:52.095 --> 00:06:56.346
Riverton Spine and Rehab billed thirty-four lines against thirty-three visits,

00:06:56.346 --> 00:07:00.761
with May 4th billed twice, so the audited figure is six thousand one hundred five

00:07:00.761 --> 00:07:01.197
dollars.

00:07:01.547 --> 00:07:03.184
Finally, prior history.

00:07:03.534 --> 00:07:07.898
John had three chiropractic visits in 2021 for low-back stiffness,

00:07:07.898 --> 00:07:10.477
resolved, and no prior neck complaints.

00:07:10.827 --> 00:07:12.893
You find that before the defense does.

00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:18.880
Before you finalize, audit the file through the defense's eyes.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.540
The slide names three value killers.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:26.555
Social media: if a client claims loss of enjoyment but posts vacation photos,

00:07:26.555 --> 00:07:29.646
the demand has to be adjusted or the client warned.

00:07:29.996 --> 00:07:32.468
That's a general check you run on every file.

00:07:32.818 --> 00:07:37.244
Then the MIST label, minor impact soft tissue, which adjusters attach when the

00:07:37.244 --> 00:07:40.818
damage is small, on the theory that low damage means no injury.

00:07:41.168 --> 00:07:43.165
Our file doesn't fit that box.

00:07:43.515 --> 00:07:47.334
Smith hit the passenger door at about twenty-eight miles an hour,

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:50.507
the Civic it was pushed into needed structural repair,

00:07:50.507 --> 00:07:53.856
and John's injury is a disc herniation documented on MRI,

00:07:53.856 --> 00:07:55.090
not just soft tissue.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.375
Still, expect the argument, and answer it with the medical narrative.

00:07:59.725 --> 00:08:02.589
Finally, the venue audit: where would suit be filed,

00:08:02.589 --> 00:08:05.948
and how does that jury pool tend to value pain and suffering?

00:08:06.298 --> 00:08:10.420
On our file that's Riverton County, and that read belongs to Atty. Thorne.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:16.557
Here is what an audited file looks like when it goes to the attorney.

00:08:16.907 --> 00:08:17.801
Three pieces.

00:08:18.151 --> 00:08:21.065
A one-page summary memo of what the audit found.

00:08:21.415 --> 00:08:24.980
An exhibit index, with every bill, record and photo labeled.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:30.124
And the Ask, the demand figure, calculated from the policy limits and the specials.

00:08:30.474 --> 00:08:35.102
On our file, the Ask is simple to state: Keystone's ten thousand dollar BI limit,

00:08:35.102 --> 00:08:39.902
against total specials of sixteen thousand four hundred one dollars and forty cents.

00:08:40.252 --> 00:08:44.826
The specials alone exceed the limit, before pain and suffering is even counted.

00:08:45.176 --> 00:08:49.298
Now the pro tip on the slide: audit the policy limits one more time.

00:08:49.648 --> 00:08:53.475
The slide's example of a hundred thousand dollar demand against a twenty-five

00:08:53.475 --> 00:08:56.358
thousand dollar policy is general, but the lesson fits us.

00:08:56.708 --> 00:09:01.074
Keystone gave its limit verbally at first and wouldn't confirm it in writing until

00:09:01.074 --> 00:09:02.618
it had signed authorizations.

00:09:02.968 --> 00:09:08.447
The Declaration Page confirming ten thousand dollars arrived March 20th, 2026.

00:09:08.797 --> 00:09:10.922
Never build a demand on a verbal number.

00:09:13.472 --> 00:09:15.655
Now we move into the Demand phase.

00:09:16.005 --> 00:09:20.554
A Bodily Injury demand pulls together legal, medical and negotiation skills,

00:09:20.554 --> 00:09:25.402
and the slide's point is that a Case Manager has to understand the why behind the

00:09:25.402 --> 00:09:26.001
paperwork.

00:09:26.351 --> 00:09:27.918
Three foundations hold it up.

00:09:28.268 --> 00:09:32.455
The statute of limitations: ours is docketed for February 6th,

00:09:32.455 --> 00:09:37.115
2028, so we're well inside it, but it's on the calendar from day one.

00:09:37.465 --> 00:09:42.059
The liability framework: Robert Smith was cited for running the stop sign,

00:09:42.059 --> 00:09:44.605
we have an independent witness and video.

00:09:44.955 --> 00:09:49.256
And the coverage analysis: our recovery stack is forty thousand dollars,

00:09:49.256 --> 00:09:54.275
Keystone's ten thousand in BI first, then Harbor Point's twenty thousand in Host UM,

00:09:54.275 --> 00:09:57.262
then Cardinal State's ten thousand in Relative UM.

00:09:57.612 --> 00:10:02.348
That order matters, because the UM layers can't open until BI is exhausted.

00:10:02.698 --> 00:10:06.483
So the demand to Keystone is the key that unlocks everything behind it.

00:10:09.033 --> 00:10:10.844
This slide is about mindset.

00:10:11.194 --> 00:10:14.863
In the Demand phase you stop reacting and start setting the stage.

00:10:15.213 --> 00:10:17.059
Think in terms of the policy limit.

00:10:17.409 --> 00:10:21.725
The goal isn't to mail a stack of records, it's to present the case so the adjuster

00:10:21.725 --> 00:10:23.493
sees real risk in front of a jury.

00:10:23.843 --> 00:10:25.271
Two checks come first.

00:10:25.621 --> 00:10:29.184
Maximum Medical Improvement: make sure treatment is complete,

00:10:29.184 --> 00:10:31.286
or there's a clear future care plan.

00:10:31.636 --> 00:10:35.165
On our file, Dr. Reyes declared MMI on June 12th,

00:10:35.165 --> 00:10:40.638
2026, with residual tingling in the right arm and possibly one or two repeat

00:10:40.638 --> 00:10:42.654
injections within two years.

00:10:43.004 --> 00:10:47.172
That future care is described in the narrative, not counted in the specials.

00:10:47.522 --> 00:10:52.166
Then the gap check: identify and explain every gap before the adjuster uses it.

00:10:52.516 --> 00:10:56.756
For us that means the fourteen-day initial gap and the nineteen-day disruption in

00:10:56.756 --> 00:11:00.735
April, both explained and corroborated, and addressed up front in the demand

00:11:00.735 --> 00:11:01.154
package.

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:08.174
Let's look closer at treatment gaps, because ours will be the adjuster's main

00:11:08.174 --> 00:11:08.580
weapon.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:12.137
Adjusters use gaps to argue the injury wasn't serious,

00:11:12.137 --> 00:11:15.463
the treatment wasn't needed, the client recovered early,

00:11:15.463 --> 00:11:17.661
or something else caused the problem.

00:11:18.011 --> 00:11:20.379
An unexplained gap costs real money.

00:11:20.729 --> 00:11:24.579
So your strategy is to find gaps early, document the reason for each,

00:11:24.579 --> 00:11:28.763
gather the support, and put the explanation right into the demand timeline.

00:11:29.113 --> 00:11:32.688
When you address it, acknowledge it plainly, give the context,

00:11:32.688 --> 00:11:35.975
and show the complaints were consistent before and after.

00:11:36.325 --> 00:11:39.181
On our file, the demand handles the gap up front.

00:11:39.531 --> 00:11:43.320
The EMS report shows neck stiffness on the day of the crash,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:47.929
and Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter of June 19th, 2026 explains that delayed

00:11:47.929 --> 00:11:53.171
presentation is clinically consistent with a disc injury whose symptoms evolve over

00:11:53.171 --> 00:11:54.055
days to weeks.

00:11:54.405 --> 00:11:58.782
A well-explained gap does far less damage than one the adjuster discovers.

00:12:01.332 --> 00:12:05.521
Before the attorney reviews the demand letter, the Case Manager makes sure the Big

00:12:05.521 --> 00:12:06.440
Four are flawless.

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:10.021
Medical specials: a clean, itemized list of every provider,

00:12:10.021 --> 00:12:14.294
date and amount, which for us totals fourteen thousand eight hundred seventeen

00:12:14.294 --> 00:12:16.484
dollars and forty cents after the audit.

00:12:16.834 --> 00:12:20.933
The narrative: the human story of how the injury changed the client's life.

00:12:21.283 --> 00:12:24.932
John's signed Personal Impact Statement tells it in his own words,

00:12:24.932 --> 00:12:29.412
including the panic attack at his own corner, the nightmares and giving up pickup

00:12:29.412 --> 00:12:32.951
basketball, and whether that's presented is Atty. Thorne's call.

00:12:33.301 --> 00:12:36.218
Liability proof: the police report citing Smith,

00:12:36.218 --> 00:12:39.257
Ruth Calloway's statement, and the doorbell video.

00:12:39.607 --> 00:12:43.774
And special damages: wage verification and out-of-pocket receipts.

00:12:44.124 --> 00:12:48.366
We have Northgate's letter supporting one thousand five hundred eighty-four dollars

00:12:48.366 --> 00:12:52.607
in lost wages, and Riverton Drug receipts of three hundred twelve dollars and forty

00:12:52.607 --> 00:12:52.913
cents.

00:12:53.263 --> 00:12:55.643
All four pieces have to agree with each other.

00:12:58.193 --> 00:13:01.920
Then the final scrub, which catches the traps adjusters look for.

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:05.590
Billing overlaps first: no double billing for the same date.

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:09.667
That's the May 4th duplicate on Riverton Spine and Rehab's ledger.

00:13:10.017 --> 00:13:14.236
The clinic said it would only reissue the ledger when the LOP account closes,

00:13:14.236 --> 00:13:18.400
so the demand went out on the audited six thousand one hundred five dollars,

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.153
supported by the attendance log.

00:13:20.503 --> 00:13:22.581
Second, pre-existing conditions.

00:13:22.931 --> 00:13:26.658
The slide's advice is to show aggravation rather than hide history.

00:13:27.008 --> 00:13:30.527
On our file the history is low back in 2021, resolved,

00:13:30.527 --> 00:13:35.286
and the injury now is in the neck, so you present it openly as unrelated.

00:13:35.635 --> 00:13:40.344
Third, balance verification: confirm every provider's balance so the numbers are

00:13:40.344 --> 00:13:40.697
exact.

00:13:41.047 --> 00:13:45.843
Crescent's statement wrongly billed John one thousand five hundred forty dollars it

00:13:45.843 --> 00:13:49.887
couldn't legally charge him, and the corrected statement of July 10th,

00:13:49.887 --> 00:13:50.811
2026 shows zero.

00:13:51.161 --> 00:13:53.507
That's the scrub catching a real error.

00:13:56.057 --> 00:14:00.259
This slide is for senior Case Managers who handle the first call with the adjuster.

00:14:00.609 --> 00:14:01.527
Two skills.

00:14:01.876 --> 00:14:05.156
The anchor technique: never apologize for a high demand,

00:14:05.156 --> 00:14:07.205
and stay firm on what drives value.

00:14:07.555 --> 00:14:10.999
And note-taking: write down every reason the adjuster gives,

00:14:10.999 --> 00:14:15.706
such as low impact or delayed treatment, because those notes become the attorney's

00:14:15.706 --> 00:14:17.772
ammunition if the case goes to suit.

00:14:18.122 --> 00:14:21.605
On our file, you can see why that second point matters.

00:14:21.955 --> 00:14:25.208
When Keystone made its offer on August 5th, 2026,

00:14:25.208 --> 00:14:30.519
it cited two things: the fourteen-day gap and a claim that the MRI findings were

00:14:30.519 --> 00:14:31.382
degenerative.

00:14:31.732 --> 00:14:34.356
Those are the excuses you log word for word.

00:14:34.706 --> 00:14:37.551
They told us exactly what the rebuttal had to answer,

00:14:37.551 --> 00:14:41.684
and the next day the rebuttal Atty. Thorne approved went out addressing both.

00:14:42.034 --> 00:14:45.534
Remember that strategy and settlement figures are the attorney's;

00:14:45.534 --> 00:14:47.688
your job is accuracy and a clean record.

00:14:50.238 --> 00:14:52.606
Here are the best practices for the demand.

00:14:52.956 --> 00:14:56.090
The thirty-day rule says that once a client reaches MMI,

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:58.552
the demand should be out within thirty days.

00:14:58.902 --> 00:15:04.283
On our file, MMI was June 12th, 2026, and the Time-Limited Demand went out July

00:15:04.283 --> 00:15:09.801
20th, after the Causation Letter, the wage verification and the corrected records

00:15:09.801 --> 00:15:10.686
were in hand.

00:15:11.036 --> 00:15:15.262
So treat the thirty days as a target, and document why a file needs longer.

00:15:15.612 --> 00:15:19.409
Photo impact: lead with the strongest image to set the tone.

00:15:19.759 --> 00:15:24.034
The why factor: every bill needs a reason the treatment was necessary,

00:15:24.034 --> 00:15:27.821
which for us runs from urgent care, to the orthopedic consult,

00:15:27.821 --> 00:15:29.592
to the MRI, to the injection.

00:15:29.942 --> 00:15:33.464
And the pro tip is a mindset: a demand isn't a request for money,

00:15:33.464 --> 00:15:35.469
it's the presentation of a debt owed.

00:15:35.819 --> 00:15:40.950
Our demand asked for the limits, with a Limited Release of Robert Smith and Keystone

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:44.004
only, and Smith's affidavit of no other insurance.

00:15:46.554 --> 00:15:48.377
Now settlement negotiations.

00:15:48.726 --> 00:15:52.528
The demand letter is your opening move, and if it reads like a template,

00:15:52.528 --> 00:15:54.218
the adjuster treats it like one.

00:15:54.568 --> 00:15:56.878
The slide's battle map has four steps.

00:15:57.228 --> 00:16:00.439
First, the net sheet: add up liens, costs and fees,

00:16:00.439 --> 00:16:04.090
so you know the floor below which the client gets nothing.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:08.960
On our file, costs are four hundred eighty-six dollars and seventy-five cents and

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.638
the fee is one-third pre-suit, before the liens.

00:16:11.988 --> 00:16:15.819
Second, value drivers, the facts that would move a jury.

00:16:16.169 --> 00:16:18.271
The slide's examples are general.

00:16:18.621 --> 00:16:21.933
Ours are a defendant cited for running a stop sign,

00:16:21.933 --> 00:16:25.375
an independent witness, video of him rolling through,

00:16:25.375 --> 00:16:27.258
and a disc herniation on MRI.

00:16:27.608 --> 00:16:31.567
Third, anticipate the adjuster: name your two weaknesses.

00:16:31.917 --> 00:16:35.017
Ours are the fourteen-day gap and the April disruption.

00:16:35.367 --> 00:16:37.980
Fourth, prepare the rebuttal before they raise it.

00:16:38.329 --> 00:16:42.521
That's why the Causation Letter and the gap explanation were already in the package.

00:16:45.071 --> 00:16:47.369
This slide introduces the set-off.

00:16:47.719 --> 00:16:50.918
In many jurisdictions, PIP pays regardless of fault,

00:16:50.918 --> 00:16:54.609
and the defendant's carrier gets a credit for what PIP paid,

00:16:54.609 --> 00:16:57.808
on the theory that the law prevents double recovery.

00:16:58.158 --> 00:17:03.127
So gross settlement value minus the PIP paid equals the defendant's remaining

00:17:03.127 --> 00:17:03.708
exposure.

00:17:04.058 --> 00:17:08.121
That's general information worth knowing, and you'll meet it on other files.

00:17:08.471 --> 00:17:11.583
But our file runs on Riverton's rule, and it's different.

00:17:11.933 --> 00:17:14.266
Riverton is an add-on PIP state.

00:17:14.616 --> 00:17:20.175
PIP does not reduce BI or UM, and the PIP carrier can't recover what it paid out of

00:17:20.175 --> 00:17:21.849
its insured's settlement.

00:17:22.199 --> 00:17:25.685
Harbor Point paid five thousand dollars in PIP for John,

00:17:25.685 --> 00:17:30.540
and confirmed in writing on March 2nd, 2026 that it has no reimbursement right

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:31.847
against his recovery.

00:17:32.197 --> 00:17:35.158
So on our file there's no set-off against Keystone.

00:17:35.508 --> 00:17:39.525
Always check which rule your jurisdiction follows before you do this math.

00:17:42.075 --> 00:17:45.983
Adjusters look at PIP payments to gauge how serious a claim is,

00:17:45.983 --> 00:17:48.588
and this slide shows why that can mislead.

00:17:48.938 --> 00:17:53.545
Its example is a ten thousand dollar PIP limit used up in forty-eight hours on

00:17:53.545 --> 00:17:57.738
emergency room and E M S bills, with the specials running far above it.

00:17:58.088 --> 00:18:02.506
The lesson is in the gap: how fast PIP ran out tells you about the bills,

00:18:02.506 --> 00:18:03.777
not about the injury.

00:18:04.127 --> 00:18:06.718
What PIP paid is never the measure of the case;

00:18:06.718 --> 00:18:11.128
the full specials are what anchor the value of the claim for pain and suffering.

00:18:11.478 --> 00:18:16.323
When you reach the Think it through challenge, you will work this out on John Doe's

00:18:16.323 --> 00:18:20.058
own file: whose PIP he claims, what it paid and when it ran out,

00:18:20.058 --> 00:18:22.101
and how his total specials compare.

00:18:22.451 --> 00:18:25.993
Keep the slide's method in mind and bring the file's documents,

00:18:25.993 --> 00:18:27.455
not the example's numbers.

00:18:30.005 --> 00:18:33.916
This slide talks about the Emergency Medical Condition finding,

00:18:33.916 --> 00:18:35.903
the EMC, as a negotiating lever.

00:18:36.253 --> 00:18:40.866
In a state with an EMC rule, such as Florida, PIP benefits are capped at a lower

00:18:40.866 --> 00:18:44.038
amount unless a qualifying provider makes that finding,

00:18:44.038 --> 00:18:46.748
which pushes more bills onto the BI settlement.

00:18:47.098 --> 00:18:52.137
The slide's example, including its doctor, is general and not from our file.

00:18:52.487 --> 00:18:54.240
Here's what matters for John.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.529
Riverton has no EMC rule.

00:18:56.879 --> 00:19:01.638
There's no treat-within-fourteen-days condition and no EMC determination capping his

00:19:01.638 --> 00:19:06.283
PIP, so his full five thousand dollars was available despite the fourteen-day gap.

00:19:06.633 --> 00:19:09.431
That's why we teach EMC as a contrast.

00:19:09.781 --> 00:19:13.206
If you work a Florida file, you'll have to chase that finding.

00:19:13.556 --> 00:19:17.831
On ours, the gap matters for causation arguments with Keystone,

00:19:17.831 --> 00:19:19.256
not for PIP benefits.

00:19:19.606 --> 00:19:23.263
Knowing the difference keeps you from making an argument that doesn't apply.

00:19:25.813 --> 00:19:28.871
Here's a technical point that changes the client's net:

00:19:28.871 --> 00:19:30.817
subrogation versus non-subrogation.

00:19:31.167 --> 00:19:35.478
Under non-subrogation, the PIP carrier can't take back what it paid from the BI

00:19:35.478 --> 00:19:38.807
settlement, and that money effectively stays with the client.

00:19:39.157 --> 00:19:42.673
Under subrogation, the carrier places a lien on the settlement,

00:19:42.673 --> 00:19:45.240
and the Case Manager has to negotiate it down.

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:48.632
The slide's ten thousand dollar figure is a general example.

00:19:48.982 --> 00:19:52.747
On our file, Harbor Point's PIP falls on the non-subrogation side:

00:19:52.747 --> 00:19:57.253
it confirmed in writing that it has no reimbursement right on the five thousand

00:19:57.253 --> 00:19:58.165
dollars it paid.

00:19:58.515 --> 00:19:59.793
But don't stop there.

00:20:00.142 --> 00:20:02.372
A health plan can be a different story.

00:20:02.722 --> 00:20:07.403
Peach State asserted a reimbursement right on its first notice and paid one thousand

00:20:07.403 --> 00:20:09.966
eight hundred sixty dollars for the injection.

00:20:10.316 --> 00:20:14.682
That one is a real lien, and it's the one we have to resolve before John is paid.

00:20:17.231 --> 00:20:22.352
The slide describes strategic exhaustion: spend PIP on the hard costs like imaging,

00:20:22.352 --> 00:20:27.473
then move later care to Letters of Protection, because LOP providers are often more

00:20:27.473 --> 00:20:30.002
willing to reduce their bills at the end.

00:20:30.352 --> 00:20:34.207
On our file, it worked a little differently but the logic is the same.

00:20:34.557 --> 00:20:39.682
PIP paid the urgent care visit, Dr. Reyes's visits and the twenty-three hundred

00:20:39.682 --> 00:20:41.175
ninety-five dollar MRI.

00:20:41.525 --> 00:20:45.877
Riverton Spine and Rehab was out of network for Peach State,

00:20:45.877 --> 00:20:49.939
so John treated there under an LOP signed February 23rd,

00:20:49.939 --> 00:20:51.463
2026, from the start.

00:20:51.813 --> 00:20:54.791
And that LOP balance is the one that came down at the end,

00:20:54.791 --> 00:20:59.104
from the audited six thousand one hundred five dollars to four thousand five hundred

00:20:59.104 --> 00:20:59.464
eighty.

00:20:59.814 --> 00:21:03.691
Now the tip: verify the PIP ledger before you negotiate.

00:21:04.041 --> 00:21:07.884
Ours shows five payments totaling exactly five thousand dollars.

00:21:08.234 --> 00:21:11.671
Quote the specials accurately and you protect your credibility.

00:21:14.221 --> 00:21:17.808
Negotiations begin once the demand is in the adjuster's hands.

00:21:18.158 --> 00:21:22.504
Start with the soft lead: confirm they received the demand and every record,

00:21:22.504 --> 00:21:25.020
and point them to the evidence that matters.

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:30.841
On our file, Dana Whitfield at Keystone confirmed receipt in writing on July 22nd,

00:21:30.841 --> 00:21:34.845
2026, and that acknowledgement started the thirty-day clock,

00:21:34.845 --> 00:21:37.781
so the acceptance window ran to August 21st.

00:21:38.131 --> 00:21:41.126
Then set the tone: the file is litigation ready.

00:21:41.476 --> 00:21:43.241
Next, the ask.

00:21:43.591 --> 00:21:46.898
The slide's script mentions forty thousand dollars in specials,

00:21:46.898 --> 00:21:48.316
which is a general example.

00:21:48.666 --> 00:21:53.367
On ours, you'd say clear liability and sixteen thousand four hundred one dollars and

00:21:53.367 --> 00:21:56.781
forty cents in specials, against a ten thousand dollar limit.

00:21:57.131 --> 00:22:01.183
And when the lowball comes, stay calm and use the professional pause.

00:22:01.533 --> 00:22:06.235
Keystone's offer of three thousand five hundred dollars on August 5th was exactly

00:22:06.235 --> 00:22:06.932
that moment.

00:22:07.282 --> 00:22:10.695
Ask what data supports the number, and log the answer.

00:22:13.245 --> 00:22:17.192
Bracketing is a technique for when the two sides seem impossibly far apart.

00:22:17.542 --> 00:22:21.111
The slide's numbers, a hundred thousand against thirty thousand,

00:22:21.111 --> 00:22:22.337
are a general example.

00:22:22.687 --> 00:22:26.198
You propose a logic: if I can move my side into one range,

00:22:26.198 --> 00:22:27.772
can you move into another?

00:22:28.122 --> 00:22:33.092
You check authority: if the adjuster says they can't go that high, you ask who can.

00:22:33.441 --> 00:22:37.168
And once you're both inside the bracket, you negotiate toward the middle.

00:22:37.518 --> 00:22:40.154
It's a useful tool on files with room to move.

00:22:40.504 --> 00:22:43.662
On our file, though, notice why it doesn't really apply.

00:22:44.012 --> 00:22:47.599
John's specials alone exceed Keystone's ten thousand dollar limit,

00:22:47.599 --> 00:22:51.784
so the demand was for the limits, and there's nowhere above the limit for the

00:22:51.784 --> 00:22:52.545
carrier to go.

00:22:52.895 --> 00:22:56.721
Keystone's three thousand five hundred offer was answered with a rebuttal,

00:22:56.721 --> 00:22:57.342
not a split.

00:22:57.692 --> 00:23:02.765
Any move off the limits would be Atty. Thorne's decision, never the Case Manager's.

00:23:05.315 --> 00:23:09.773
Adjusters use stall tactics to wear you down, so you'll accept less.

00:23:10.123 --> 00:23:13.014
The slide gives three, each with a rebuttal.

00:23:13.364 --> 00:23:18.078
The first is waiting on a manager's authority: answer with a clear, polite deadline.

00:23:18.428 --> 00:23:23.239
The second is asking for old records: say they're not relevant to this acute injury,

00:23:23.239 --> 00:23:26.218
and ask for a good-faith offer on what's undisputed.

00:23:26.568 --> 00:23:29.930
The third is that the damage was too low for the injury:

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:33.351
answer that low-speed impacts can still injure the spine,

00:23:33.351 --> 00:23:34.672
and the MRI proves it.

00:23:35.022 --> 00:23:38.540
On our file, Keystone's argument was close to that third one.

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:41.629
It called the MRI findings degenerative.

00:23:41.979 --> 00:23:44.452
And our rebuttal came straight from the record.

00:23:44.802 --> 00:23:49.953
The radiologist, Dr. Helen Park, wrote that there was no significant degenerative

00:23:49.953 --> 00:23:51.861
change at the adjacent levels.

00:23:52.211 --> 00:23:56.227
Pair that with Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter and the EMS report,

00:23:56.227 --> 00:23:58.585
and the stall has an answer on paper.

00:24:01.135 --> 00:24:04.305
The last stretch of a negotiation is often the hardest.

00:24:04.654 --> 00:24:08.709
The slide's split-the-difference script, with its forty-five thousand dollar

00:24:08.709 --> 00:24:11.644
figures, is a general example of closing the final gap.

00:24:11.994 --> 00:24:15.140
What applies to every file is what you do before you hang up.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:16.755
Confirm the terms.

00:24:17.105 --> 00:24:19.021
Is this Bodily Injury only?

00:24:19.371 --> 00:24:21.844
Is any property damage claim still open?

00:24:22.194 --> 00:24:23.285
What about liens?

00:24:23.635 --> 00:24:25.087
When will the check issue?

00:24:25.436 --> 00:24:28.908
Then create the paper trail with a confirming email right away.

00:24:29.258 --> 00:24:32.253
On our file, each of those questions had a real answer.

00:24:32.603 --> 00:24:37.595
Keystone tendered its ten thousand dollar limits on August 12th, 2026.

00:24:37.945 --> 00:24:42.218
It was BI only, because Margaret's Civic claim stayed open on its own.

00:24:42.568 --> 00:24:45.354
The liens stayed with the firm to resolve from trust.

00:24:45.704 --> 00:24:49.013
And the release terms had to protect the UM claims behind it.

00:24:49.363 --> 00:24:53.833
Getting those terms in writing is what keeps the forty thousand dollar stack intact.

00:24:56.383 --> 00:24:59.030
Finally, the never rule, three of them.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:04.117
Never apologize for a high demand, because it's based on the client's real losses.

00:25:04.467 --> 00:25:08.889
Never bid against yourself: once you've moved, the adjuster has to move before you

00:25:08.889 --> 00:25:09.482
move again.

00:25:09.832 --> 00:25:13.581
And never accept that's all I have as the final answer on the first day,

00:25:13.581 --> 00:25:16.705
because every adjuster has a supervisor with more authority.

00:25:17.055 --> 00:25:19.563
On our file you can see all three at work.

00:25:19.913 --> 00:25:24.104
The demand asked for Keystone's limits, and the specials justified it.

00:25:24.454 --> 00:25:27.622
When Keystone offered three thousand five hundred dollars,

00:25:27.622 --> 00:25:31.664
the response wasn't a lower counter, it was the approved rebuttal with the

00:25:31.664 --> 00:25:34.996
radiologist's words, the Causation Letter and the EMS report.

00:25:35.346 --> 00:25:41.151
And Keystone did move, tendering the full ten thousand dollars on August 12th, 2026.

00:25:41.501 --> 00:25:44.473
Holding your position with evidence is what got there.

00:25:47.023 --> 00:25:50.262
Now we reach the BI settlement, and the UM safety check.

00:25:50.612 --> 00:25:53.543
When the at-fault driver has low or no coverage,

00:25:53.543 --> 00:25:57.451
the client looks to Uninsured or Underinsured Motorist coverage.

00:25:57.801 --> 00:26:02.382
The danger the slide warns about is real: settle with the at-fault driver and sign a

00:26:02.382 --> 00:26:06.473
release without the UM carrier's consent, and you can forfeit the UM claim.

00:26:06.823 --> 00:26:11.672
So you notify the UM carrier of the offer and get its written consent and waiver of

00:26:11.672 --> 00:26:12.373
subrogation.

00:26:12.723 --> 00:26:15.625
On our file this protects thirty thousand dollars.

00:26:15.975 --> 00:26:20.754
After Keystone tendered, consent-to-settle requests went to Harbor Point and

00:26:20.754 --> 00:26:26.036
Cardinal State on August 13th, 2026, each with a thirty-day window and the option to

00:26:26.036 --> 00:26:29.118
substitute its own check to preserve subrogation.

00:26:29.468 --> 00:26:33.879
Harbor Point consented August 24th and Cardinal State August 26th.

00:26:34.229 --> 00:26:38.548
Bluewater wasn't asked, because Margaret's Civic policy has no UM.

00:26:38.898 --> 00:26:41.975
Only with both consents in hand did the signing go ahead.

00:26:44.525 --> 00:26:48.159
Three legal doctrines shape how a settlement and its liens resolve.

00:26:48.509 --> 00:26:52.675
The made whole doctrine says a health insurer can't take from a settlement unless

00:26:52.675 --> 00:26:56.687
the client has been fully compensated, which can help persuade a lienholder to

00:26:56.687 --> 00:26:58.435
reduce when the recovery is small.

00:26:58.785 --> 00:27:03.145
The common fund doctrine says a lienholder should reduce its claim by the attorney's

00:27:03.145 --> 00:27:05.740
fee share, since it benefits from the firm's work.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:09.764
And the statute of limitations is the deadline that can end a case,

00:27:09.764 --> 00:27:11.245
so you track it constantly.

00:27:11.594 --> 00:27:15.064
On our file, Peach State is a fully insured group plan,

00:27:15.064 --> 00:27:18.660
so state law governs any reduction, not ERISA preemption,

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:20.615
and that's Atty. Thorne's call.

00:27:20.965 --> 00:27:25.396
Its one thousand eight hundred sixty dollar lien was accepted at one thousand two

00:27:25.396 --> 00:27:28.349
hundred forty, a one-third procurement-cost reduction,

00:27:28.349 --> 00:27:30.428
which is the common fund idea at work.

00:27:30.778 --> 00:27:35.642
And our statute date stays docketed for February 6th, 2028.

00:27:38.192 --> 00:27:42.566
Now the execution, starting with the release, the document that legally ends the

00:27:42.566 --> 00:27:42.894
claim.

00:27:43.244 --> 00:27:47.863
The slide calls it a Release of All Claims, and it's the most important document in

00:27:47.863 --> 00:27:48.364
the file.

00:27:48.714 --> 00:27:51.721
Make sure the client isn't signing away something still open.

00:27:52.071 --> 00:27:56.306
Know the non-admission of liability language, and read the indemnity and hold

00:27:56.306 --> 00:27:57.737
harmless clause carefully.

00:27:58.087 --> 00:28:00.234
Our file shows exactly why.

00:28:00.584 --> 00:28:05.243
Keystone's draft of August 14th, 2026 released all other persons and insurers,

00:28:05.243 --> 00:28:08.707
which would have released Harbor Point and Cardinal State,

00:28:08.707 --> 00:28:10.917
thirty thousand dollars of the stack.

00:28:11.267 --> 00:28:15.521
It also released property damage, though Margaret's Civic claim was open,

00:28:15.521 --> 00:28:20.300
and it carried a confidentiality clause and a lien indemnity with attorney's fees.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:23.680
The Case Manager flagged each clause to Atty. Thorne.

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:28.895
His amendments were accepted, and Keystone issued a Limited Release covering Robert

00:28:28.895 --> 00:28:33.701
Smith and Keystone only, BI only, with the UM and Civic claims expressly reserved.

00:28:34.051 --> 00:28:36.187
John signed it August 28th.

00:28:38.737 --> 00:28:41.884
The Settlement Disclosure Statement is the truth document.

00:28:42.234 --> 00:28:45.926
It may be required by state law or by the firm's compliance rules,

00:28:45.926 --> 00:28:50.233
and its job is to make sure the client understands exactly what is happening.

00:28:50.583 --> 00:28:53.420
It explains that the settlement is full and final,

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:57.108
so even if symptoms flare later, there's no coming back for more.

00:28:57.458 --> 00:29:00.131
And in some states there's a cooling-off period,

00:29:00.131 --> 00:29:03.362
a few business days when the client can change their mind,

00:29:03.362 --> 00:29:06.815
and the Case Manager tracks that date before any check is cut.

00:29:07.165 --> 00:29:10.195
On our file, this connects to John's future care.

00:29:10.545 --> 00:29:14.806
Dr. Reyes said he may need one or two more injections within two years.

00:29:15.156 --> 00:29:19.605
That's exactly the kind of thing the client has to understand before the file

00:29:19.605 --> 00:29:20.009
closes.

00:29:20.359 --> 00:29:24.405
John signed his Settlement Statement on October 21st,

00:29:24.405 --> 00:29:28.068
2026, and disbursement followed on October 22nd.

00:29:28.418 --> 00:29:32.319
Whether a rescission period applies is something Atty. Thorne confirms.

00:29:34.869 --> 00:29:37.249
Lien payoff letters are your verification.

00:29:37.599 --> 00:29:41.892
You never pay a lien off a bill alone; you need a formal final demand or payoff

00:29:41.892 --> 00:29:42.870
letter in writing.

00:29:43.220 --> 00:29:46.517
The slide's Medicare example is important general knowledge.

00:29:46.867 --> 00:29:50.201
For a client who is sixty-five or older or on disability,

00:29:50.201 --> 00:29:54.061
Medicare issues a final demand that can take more than sixty days,

00:29:54.061 --> 00:29:56.167
and the file waits until it arrives.

00:29:56.517 --> 00:30:01.497
On our file, that check was run on Day 2, and John is not a Medicare beneficiary.

00:30:01.847 --> 00:30:05.272
Our liens are Peach State and Riverton Spine and Rehab.

00:30:05.622 --> 00:30:10.279
Peach State accepted one thousand two hundred forty dollars in writing on October

00:30:10.279 --> 00:30:14.993
9th, 2026, and Riverton Spine and Rehab accepted four thousand five hundred eighty

00:30:14.993 --> 00:30:16.373
dollars on October 12th.

00:30:16.723 --> 00:30:20.952
Once each is paid, get a signed letter confirming the lien is satisfied,

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:23.654
so no provider ever sends John to collections.

00:30:26.204 --> 00:30:30.616
The Settlement Statement is the net sheet, the ledger that takes the settlement from

00:30:30.616 --> 00:30:32.926
gross to net, and you audit it line by line.

00:30:33.276 --> 00:30:35.958
First, every case cost needs a receipt.

00:30:36.308 --> 00:30:40.156
On our file, costs through settlement were four hundred eighty-six dollars and

00:30:40.156 --> 00:30:44.053
seventy-five cents, including twenty-five dollars for the police report and two

00:30:44.053 --> 00:30:48.000
hundred fourteen dollars and seventy-five cents for medical records and billing.

00:30:48.350 --> 00:30:49.603
Then the net to client.

00:30:49.953 --> 00:30:55.062
On Track A, the gross was forty thousand dollars across BI and both UM layers.

00:30:55.412 --> 00:30:59.436
Less the one-third fee of thirteen thousand three hundred thirty-three dollars and

00:30:59.436 --> 00:31:03.362
thirty-three cents, the costs, and the reduced liens of one thousand two hundred

00:31:03.362 --> 00:31:07.337
forty and four thousand five hundred eighty, John's net was twenty thousand three

00:31:07.337 --> 00:31:09.692
hundred fifty-nine dollars and ninety-two cents.

00:31:10.042 --> 00:31:13.197
The slide asks you to check that the net covers future care,

00:31:13.197 --> 00:31:17.455
and Dr. Reyes put that between three thousand four hundred and six thousand eight

00:31:17.455 --> 00:31:18.297
hundred dollars.

00:31:20.847 --> 00:31:23.726
The dismissal is the court filing that ends a lawsuit.

00:31:24.076 --> 00:31:28.186
If suit was filed, the settlement isn't finished until the court is told.

00:31:28.536 --> 00:31:32.541
Dismissal with prejudice means the case is closed and can't be refiled.

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:37.106
Dismissal without prejudice, which is rare in settlements, leaves the door open.

00:31:37.456 --> 00:31:40.892
So the Case Manager checks that with prejudice is the language used.

00:31:41.242 --> 00:31:43.634
On our file, this depends on the track.

00:31:43.984 --> 00:31:47.301
On Track A, the case settled before any suit was filed,

00:31:47.301 --> 00:31:49.232
so there was nothing to dismiss.

00:31:49.581 --> 00:31:54.504
On Track B, which we follow on Days 4 and 5, suit was filed in Riverton County,

00:31:54.504 --> 00:31:57.744
and after Keystone tendered its limits at mediation,

00:31:57.744 --> 00:31:59.427
Robert Smith was dismissed.

00:31:59.777 --> 00:32:04.458
That's when this slide becomes your checklist: confirm the dismissal language before

00:32:04.458 --> 00:32:05.350
the file closes.

00:32:07.899 --> 00:32:13.124
Many carriers won't issue a check until they have a W-9 from the firm or the payee.

00:32:13.474 --> 00:32:17.386
If you don't request it the moment terms are agreed, expect a delay.

00:32:17.736 --> 00:32:20.024
The slide compares the key documents.

00:32:20.374 --> 00:32:23.613
The release is signed by the claimant and ends the right to sue.

00:32:23.963 --> 00:32:28.317
The final demand comes from the lienholder and states exactly what clears the debt.

00:32:28.666 --> 00:32:32.695
The W-9 is signed by the payee for tax reporting to the IRS.

00:32:33.045 --> 00:32:36.947
And the stipulation to dismiss is signed by the attorneys and tells the court the

00:32:36.947 --> 00:32:37.573
case is over.

00:32:37.923 --> 00:32:40.442
On our file, the timing shows how these fit.

00:32:40.792 --> 00:32:45.719
Keystone's Limited Release promised payment within fourteen days of receiving the

00:32:45.719 --> 00:32:47.909
executed release and the firm's W-9.

00:32:48.259 --> 00:32:54.380
On August 28th, 2026, the Case Manager emailed Dana Whitfield the signed release,

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:56.572
both UM consents and the W-9.

00:32:56.922 --> 00:33:01.438
Keystone's check issued September 1st and was deposited to trust on September 4th.

00:33:03.988 --> 00:33:08.850
Once the terms are settled, the Case Manager moves into the technical closing stage:

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:12.092
from settled to paid and archived, with full compliance.

00:33:12.442 --> 00:33:14.183
Start with document control.

00:33:14.533 --> 00:33:19.758
The signature chase: the slide mentions secure e-signature or an in-person signing.

00:33:20.108 --> 00:33:23.576
On our file, Keystone required a notarized wet-ink original,

00:33:23.576 --> 00:33:25.715
so the release could not be e-signed.

00:33:26.065 --> 00:33:30.407
The Case Manager called John on August 26th, 2026 to schedule it,

00:33:30.407 --> 00:33:35.750
logistics only: the date, the place, a government photo ID in the name John Doe,

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:38.221
and sign only in front of the notary.

00:33:38.571 --> 00:33:43.470
On August 28th, at the firm, Atty. Thorne went through the release with John first.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:48.866
Notary coordination: the slide says verify the stamp is current and the ID matches

00:33:48.866 --> 00:33:49.358
exactly.

00:33:49.708 --> 00:33:54.742
Our notary, Renee Castillo, checked John's Riverton driver's license against the

00:33:54.742 --> 00:33:56.001
name on the release.

00:33:56.351 --> 00:33:58.545
And the W-9 was sent the same day.

00:34:01.095 --> 00:34:05.159
The second set of closing tasks is final lien mitigation, the reduction.

00:34:05.509 --> 00:34:07.912
Call the billing department of every lienholder.

00:34:08.262 --> 00:34:12.970
The slide's script asks a provider to accept fifty percent as payment in full in the

00:34:12.970 --> 00:34:14.764
interest of a global resolution.

00:34:15.113 --> 00:34:19.837
That percentage is a general example, and every reduction needs Atty. Thorne's

00:34:19.837 --> 00:34:20.442
authority.

00:34:20.792 --> 00:34:24.784
On our file, Riverton Spine and Rehab's audited six thousand one hundred five

00:34:24.784 --> 00:34:27.635
dollars came down to four thousand five hundred eighty,

00:34:27.635 --> 00:34:31.781
and Peach State's one thousand eight hundred sixty came down to one thousand two

00:34:31.781 --> 00:34:32.507
hundred forty.

00:34:32.857 --> 00:34:37.234
Then update the ledger as each saving lands, so the client can see the net rising.

00:34:37.584 --> 00:34:41.557
The last item, Medicare finalization through the MSPRP portal,

00:34:41.557 --> 00:34:44.120
is general knowledge for Medicare files.

00:34:44.470 --> 00:34:49.114
John isn't a Medicare beneficiary, so on ours that step doesn't apply.

00:34:49.464 --> 00:34:54.818
The funds cleared October 20th, 2026, and the trust ledger went to zero after

00:34:54.818 --> 00:34:55.722
disbursement.

00:34:58.272 --> 00:35:00.466
That brings us to the end of Day 2.

00:35:00.816 --> 00:35:03.463
Today we audited John Doe's file before the demand,

00:35:03.463 --> 00:35:07.822
served the Time-Limited Demand to Keystone, answered the three thousand five hundred

00:35:07.822 --> 00:35:11.870
dollar offer with evidence, and walked the BI settlement through the consents,

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:13.843
the Limited Release and the net sheet.

00:35:14.193 --> 00:35:18.534
Review your notes tonight, because tomorrow we pick the file up at the demands and

00:35:18.534 --> 00:35:21.182
carry the settlement all the way to the UM layers.

00:35:21.532 --> 00:35:22.135
Thank you.

